IN THE COURT OF THE LEARNED ADDITIONAL DISTRICT JUDGE NO. __, JAIPUR CITY

Civil Suit No. ______ of 2025

Ramesh Kumar Sharma                                                      … Plaintiff

Versus

Suresh Chand Agarwal & Another                                           … Defendants

WRITTEN ARGUMENTS ON BEHALF OF THE PLAINTIFF

MOST RESPECTFULLY SHOWETH:

I. BRIEF FACTS
The Plaintiff entered into a written Agreement to Sell dated 15.01.2024 with Defendant No. 1 for a residential plot measuring 200 sq. yards for a total consideration of ₹85,00,000. A sum of ₹15,00,000 was paid by cheque which was cleared. The balance was to be paid on or before 15.07.2024 against simultaneous registration. The Plaintiff remained ready and willing, prepared a Demand Draft of the balance amount, and remained present at the Sub-Registrar’s office. Defendant No. 1 failed to perform and subsequently sold the property to Defendant No. 2 on 12.09.2024.

II. POINTS FOR DETERMINATION
1. Whether there exists a valid and enforceable Agreement to Sell dated 15.01.2024?
2. Whether the Plaintiff has always been ready and willing to perform his part of the contract as required under Section 16(c) of the Specific Relief Act, 1963?
3. Whether time was of the essence of the contract, and if so, whether the Plaintiff committed any default?
4. Whether Defendant No. 2 is a bona fide purchaser without notice under Section 19 of the Specific Relief Act?
5. Whether the Plaintiff is entitled to a decree of specific performance, permanent injunction, and/or alternative damages?

III. ARGUMENTS ON MERITS

A. Existence and Validity of the Agreement
The Agreement is in writing, signed by the parties, properly stamped, and the payment of earnest money is proved by bank records. There is no dispute on the execution of the document. The essential terms – property, price, and time – are certain. The Agreement is therefore enforceable under Section 10 of the Specific Relief Act.

B. Continuous Readiness and Willingness (Section 16(c) SRA)
It is settled law that the Plaintiff must aver and prove continuous readiness and willingness from the date of the contract till the date of the decree (see settled principles of the Hon’ble Supreme Court).  
In the present case:
- Earnest money of ₹15,00,000 was paid and cleared.
- Legal notices were issued on 05.05.2024 and 20.07.2024.
- Demand Draft of ₹70,00,000 was prepared on 10.07.2024 (before the target date).
- Plaintiff remained present at the Sub-Registrar’s office on 15.07.2024.
These facts establish continuous readiness. The defence of alleged default is unsupported by any contemporaneous document.

C. Time as Essence
Although the Agreement contains a clause that time is of the essence, the conduct of the parties is decisive. Defendant No. 1 never treated the time as rigid; he neither performed on the stipulated date nor issued any notice making time essence after alleged default. The Plaintiff was ready on the stipulated date. Hence, the defence of time being essence does not disentitle the Plaintiff.

D. Subsequent Purchaser – Section 19 SRA
Defendant No. 2 claims to be a bona fide purchaser. The burden is on Defendant No. 2 to prove that he purchased without notice of the prior Agreement and for valuable consideration. The Plaintiff has placed on record circumstances showing knowledge or constructive notice. Specific performance can therefore be enforced against Defendant No. 2 as well.

E. Discretion under Section 20 SRA
The relief of specific performance is discretionary but not arbitrary. Where the Plaintiff has performed or has been ready to perform, and the Defendant has committed breach, the Court ordinarily grants specific performance unless hardship amounting to injustice is shown. No such hardship has been pleaded or proved by the Defendants.

F. Alternative Relief
Without prejudice, the Plaintiff is entitled to refund of ₹15,00,000 together with damages, interest and costs under Section 21 of the Specific Relief Act.

IV. PRAYER
In view of the above, it is most respectfully prayed that this Hon’ble Court may be pleased to decree the suit as prayed in the plaint with costs.

